# Market Structures: Competition, Monopoly, and the Middle Ground

> Market structure classifies industries by how many sellers compete,
> how different their products are, and how hard entry is.

[definition]
**Market structure** refers to the organisational features of a market
— number of buyers and sellers, nature of the product, freedom of
entry and exit, and control over price — that shape firms' behaviour
and outcomes.
[/definition]

## 1. Perfect Competition

Features of a **perfectly competitive** market:

- Very large number of buyers and sellers — none big enough to move
  the price; each is a **price taker**.
- **Homogenous product** — identical units, perfect substitutes.
- **Free entry and exit** — no barriers; profits attract entrants
  until profits normalise.
- Perfect information and perfect mobility of factors.

The firm's demand curve is horizontal at the market price: it can sell
any quantity at that price but nothing above it. India's agricultural
commodity mandis (wheat, vegetables sold by small farmers) approximate
this structure, as does a street vegetable market.

## 2. Monopoly

A **monopoly** is one seller of a product with no close substitutes —
the firm *is* the industry and is a **price maker**. Barriers to entry
protect it: legal (patents, licences), control of a key input,
enormous capital requirement, or deliberate limit pricing.

[info title="Natural monopoly"]
When huge **economies of scale** mean one firm can serve the entire
market cheaper than two could, a **natural monopoly** results — the
average cost curve keeps falling over the whole demand range. Indian
Railways' track network and electricity transmission grids are classic
examples, which is why they are state-run or price-regulated.
[/info]

## 3. Monopolistic Competition

Many sellers, **easy entry**, but products are **differentiated** —
by brand, packaging, quality, location, service (toothpaste brands,
neighbourhood restaurants, local tuition centres). Each firm has a
slightly downward-sloping demand curve, so it has *some* pricing power
within its brand-loyal customers. Advertising and branding replace
price as the weapon; long-run profit is competed down toward normal
levels by free entry.

## 4. Oligopoly

A **few large firms** dominate — each so big that its actions affect
rivals, making firms strategically **interdependent**: before changing
price, a firm must guess rivals' reactions. Features:

- High entry barriers (capital, technology, patents).
- Products may be homogenous (steel, cement, aluminium) or
  differentiated (cars, smartphones, airlines).
- **Kinked demand curve** — rivals match a price cut but ignore a
  price rise, so price becomes sticky near the kink.
- Non-price competition and a temptation to form **cartels** (an
  explicit collusive agreement, illegal in India under the Competition
  Act, 2002).

Indian examples: telecom services, cement, two-wheelers, airlines.

@diagram tree id="market-structures-tree" layout="tree" aria="Market structures spectrum"
  "Market Structures" -> "Perfect Competition"
  "Market Structures" -> "Monopolistic Competition"
  "Market Structures" -> Oligopoly
  "Market Structures" -> Monopoly
  "Perfect Competition" -> "Many sellers, homogenous product"
  Oligopoly -> "Few large interdependent firms"
  Monopoly -> "Single seller, entry barriers"
@/diagram

## 5. Comparison of All Four Structures

| Basis | Perfect competition | Monopolistic competition | Oligopoly | Monopoly |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Number of sellers | Very many | Many | Few | One |
| Product type | Homogenous | Differentiated | Homo- or differentiated | Unique, no close substitute |
| Entry barriers | None | Low | High | Very high or absolute |
| Price control | None — price taker | Limited | Considerable, interdependent | Substantial — price maker |
| Demand curve of firm | Horizontal | Slightly downward | Kinked | Downward, market demand |
| Non-price competition | None | Branding, advertising | Heavy | Public relations |
| Approximate example | Vegetable mandis | Restaurants, toothpaste | Telecom, airlines | Indian Railways |

## 6. Price and Output Outcomes

- Perfect competition produces the largest output at the lowest
  price (P = MC); it is the efficiency benchmark.
- Monopoly restricts output below, and sets price above, the
  competitive level — creating deadweight loss, the reason for
  anti-trust and regulation of natural monopolies.
- Monopolistic competition gives variety at slightly higher prices;
  oligopoly outcomes lie between, shaped by rivalry or collusion.

[example]
When India's telecom sector consolidated to a few major players,
tariff wars, bundle pricing, and spectrum-auction bidding showed
classic oligopoly interdependence — each operator's pricing assumed
answers from the others.
[/example]

## 7. Quick Revision Table

| Clue in a question | Structure it points to |
| --- | --- |
| 'Price taker', homogenous good | Perfect competition |
| Single seller, no substitutes | Monopoly |
| Falling AC over whole market, regulation needed | Natural monopoly |
| Many sellers with brands | Monopolistic competition |
| Kinked demand, sticky prices | Oligopoly |
| Agreement among rival firms | Cartel (collusive oligopoly) |

[summary]
Sellers' numbers, product differentiation, and entry barriers place
every industry between perfect competition (price takers, benchmark
efficiency) and monopoly (price maker, deadweight loss); branding
creates the middle ground of monopolistic competition, and
interdependence defines oligopoly, where strategy — not just price —
decides outcomes.
[/summary]
